# Assignment 1.5 — GitHub Essentials II

## Objective

To upload a local project to GitHub, connect a local Git repository to a remote repository, and verify the commit history.

## 1. Create a GitHub Account

1. Open the official GitHub website: https://github.com/
2. Create a new account or sign in to an existing account.
3. Verify your email address if required.

## 2. Create a New Public Repository

1. Sign in to GitHub.
2. Click the **+** icon and select **New repository**.
3. Enter the repository name: `web-development-practice`.
4. Select **Public**.
5. Do not initialize the repository with a README file because the project already exists locally.
6. Click **Create repository**.

## 3. Open the Local Project Folder

Open the terminal in the `web-development-practice` folder.

```bash
cd web-development-practice
```

Check the current repository status:

```bash
git status
```

## 4. Connect the Local Repository to GitHub

Copy the HTTPS repository URL from your newly created GitHub repository.

It will look similar to this:

```text
https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/web-development-practice.git
```

Replace `YOUR-USERNAME` with your actual GitHub username.

Connect the local repository to GitHub:

```bash
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/web-development-practice.git
```

Verify the remote connection:

```bash
git remote -v
```

## 5. Upload the Local Project to GitHub

Rename the current branch to `main`:

```bash
git branch -M main
```

Upload the existing commits and project files:

```bash
git push -u origin main
```

If GitHub asks you to authenticate, complete the sign-in process.

After the command succeeds, refresh your GitHub repository page to verify that the files are visible.

## 6. Add a New File

Create a new file named `about.html` in the project folder.

Add the following code:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>About My Project</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>About My Project</h1>
    <p>I am learning web development and GitHub.</p>
</body>
</html>
```

Save the file.

## 7. Create the Second Commit and Push It

Add the new file to Git's staging area:

```bash
git add about.html
```

Create a commit:

```bash
git commit -m "Add about page"
```

Push the new commit to GitHub:

```bash
git push
```

## 8. Verify the Commit History on GitHub

1. Open your GitHub repository.
2. Click the **Commits** link or the commit count shown above the file list.
3. Verify that your previous commits and the new `Add about page` commit are visible.
4. Confirm that `about.html` appears in the repository file list.

You can also check the local commit history using:

```bash
git log --oneline
```

## 9. Submission

Submit the public URL of your GitHub repository.

Example:

`https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/web-development-practice`

Replace `YOUR-USERNAME` with your actual GitHub username.

## Conclusion

In this assignment, I created a public GitHub repository and connected it to my local Git repository. I uploaded my project files using `git push`, added a new HTML file, created another commit, and pushed the changes to GitHub. Finally, I verified the repository files and commit history online.
